 The given determinant can be evaluated by expanding it along the second row, which yields the following: \[\begin{vmatrix} \cos \alpha \cos \beta & \cos \alpha \sin \beta & -\sin \alpha \\ -\sin \beta & \cos \beta & 0 \\ \sin \alpha \cos \beta & \sin \alpha \sin \beta & \cos \alpha \end{vmatrix}.\]

Expanding along the second row, we get: \begin{align*}
&(-\sin \beta)\begin{vmatrix} \cos \alpha \sin \beta & -\sin \alpha \\ \sin \alpha \sin \beta & \cos \alpha \end{vmatrix} + \cos \beta \begin{vmatrix} \cos \alpha \cos \beta & -\sin \alpha \\ \sin \alpha \cos \beta & \cos \alpha \end{vmatrix}.
\end{align*}

The determinants can be calculated as follows: \begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} \cos \alpha \sin \beta & -\sin \alpha \\ \sin \alpha \sin \beta & \cos \alpha \end{vmatrix} &= (\cos \alpha \sin \beta)(\cos \alpha) - (-\sin \alpha)(\sin \alpha \sin \beta) \\
&= \cos^2 \alpha \sin \beta + \sin^2 \alpha \sin \beta \\
&= \sin \beta (\cos^2 \alpha + \sin^2 \alpha) \\
&= \sin \beta.
\end{align*}

And \begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} \cos \alpha \cos \beta & -\sin \alpha \\ \sin \alpha \cos \beta & \cos \alpha \end{vmatrix} &= (\cos \alpha \cos \beta)(\cos \alpha) - (-\sin \alpha)(\sin \alpha \cos \beta) \\
&= \cos^2 \alpha \cos \beta + \sin^2 \alpha \cos \beta \\
&= \cos \beta (\cos^2 \alpha + \sin^2 \alpha) \\
&= \cos \beta.
\end{align*}

Therefore, the determinant can be simplified to: \begin{align*}
&(-\sin \beta) \sin \beta + \cos \beta \cos \beta \\
&= -\sin^2 \beta + \cos^2 \beta \\
&= \cos^2 \beta - \sin^2 \beta.
\end{align*}

From the trigonometric identity \(\cos^2 \beta - \sin^2 \beta = \cos 2\beta\), the value of the determinant is \(\cos 2\beta\). </think> <answer> \cos 2\beta </answer>

